{%- assign cta_url = settings.primary_cta_url | default: routes.all_products_collection_url -%}
<section class="section on-cream" id="learn">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head center">
      <p class="eyebrow">Peptide education</p>
      <h2>Peptides, explained simply</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">No jargon — just the basics, so you can make an informed decision with a physician.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="edu-intro">
      <div class="edu-intro-copy">
        <h3>What are peptides?</h3>
        <p>Your body is built from <strong>proteins</strong> — picture them as long strings of beads, where each bead is an <strong>amino acid</strong>. A <strong>peptide</strong> is simply a <em>short</em> string of those same beads.</p>
        <p>Because they're small, peptides act like the body's <strong>messengers</strong> — little signals that tell cells what to do. Your body already makes thousands of them naturally, every day. In a medical setting, a physician may prescribe a specific peptide as part of a treatment plan.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="edu-viz">
        <img src="{{ 'amino.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="3D render of a molecular amino-acid chain" loading="lazy">
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="edu-grid">
      <article class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-illus"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 80" fill="none" stroke="#0C8F72" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M60 14 L80 21 V38 C80 51 71 60 60 65 C49 60 40 51 40 38 V21 Z"/><path d="M51 37 l6 6 13-13" stroke-width="2.8"/><circle cx="28" cy="24" r="2.4" fill="#57D9B6" stroke="none"/><circle cx="94" cy="48" r="2.8" fill="#57D9B6" stroke="none"/><circle cx="26" cy="54" r="1.8" fill="#7FE7CC" stroke="none"/></svg></div>
        <div class="edu-body"><h3>Are they safe?</h3><p>Safety depends on the person, the peptide, and the dose — which is exactly why every treatment here is <strong>prescription-only</strong>. An independent, licensed physician reviews your health history and decides whether a peptide is appropriate for you, and it's compounded by a licensed 503A pharmacy. Like any medication, peptides can have side effects and aren't right for everyone — that's a conversation to have with your physician.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-illus"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 80" fill="none" stroke="#0C8F72" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M75 40 A16 16 0 1 1 70 28"/><path d="M70 16 v12 h-12"/><circle cx="36" cy="56" r="3.2" fill="#0C8F72" stroke="none"/><circle cx="27" cy="58" r="2.3" fill="#57D9B6" stroke="none" opacity=".7"/><circle cx="19" cy="60" r="1.6" fill="#7FE7CC" stroke="none" opacity=".5"/></svg></div>
        <div class="edu-body"><h3>What if I stop taking them?</h3><p>Everyone responds differently, and it depends on the treatment and your daily habits. Some changes people work toward are supported by ongoing routines — sleep, nutrition, and movement — while other effects may gradually fade after stopping. There's no one-size-fits-all answer, so whether to continue, adjust, or stop is a decision to make with your physician.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-illus"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 80" fill="none" stroke="#0C8F72" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="36" y="14" width="30" height="42" rx="3"/><path d="M43 25 h16 M43 33 h16 M43 41 h10"/><circle cx="74" cy="46" r="10"/><path d="M81 53 l8 8"/></svg></div>
        <div class="edu-body"><h3>How do I know if they're right for me?</h3><p>The only way to know is a clinical evaluation. Start an online visit — an independent physician reviews your goals and health history and tells you whether treatment is appropriate. If it isn't, they'll tell you that too.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-illus"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 80" fill="none" stroke="#0C8F72" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="24" y="24" width="18" height="40" rx="4"/><path d="M26 24 v-5 h14 v5"/><path d="M24 38 h18"/><rect x="60" y="34" width="26" height="12" rx="2"/><path d="M60 40 h-9"/><path d="M51 35 v10"/><path d="M86 40 h12"/><path d="M67 34 v12 M73 34 v12 M79 34 v12"/><circle cx="99" cy="40" r="1.8" fill="#57D9B6" stroke="none"/></svg></div>
        <div class="edu-body"><h3>How is it taken?</h3><p>It depends on what's prescribed. Many peptide and GLP-1 treatments are given as a small <strong>subcutaneous self-injection</strong> — a tiny needle just under the skin, similar to insulin — usually on a weekly or daily schedule. Others may come in different forms. Your prescription ships with step-by-step instructions, and the care team can walk you through your first dose.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-illus"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 80" fill="none" stroke="#0C8F72" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M54 14 h14 M58 14 v13 L47 48 A4 4 0 0 0 51 54 h20 A4 4 0 0 0 75 48 L64 27 V14"/><path d="M50 44 h22"/><circle cx="57" cy="49" r="1.8" fill="#57D9B6" stroke="none"/><circle cx="65" cy="46" r="1.3" fill="#7FE7CC" stroke="none"/><circle cx="30" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="40" cy="18" r="2.4"/><path d="M32.5 22.5 37.5 19.5"/></svg></div>
        <div class="edu-body"><h3>How are they made?</h3><p>Prescribed treatments are prepared — or <strong>compounded</strong> — by licensed <strong>503A compounding pharmacies</strong> in the U.S. that follow strict quality, sterility, and testing standards. Compounding lets a pharmacy prepare a medication to a physician's specific prescription.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-illus"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 80" fill="none" stroke="#0C8F72" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="30" cy="30" r="4"/><circle cx="44" cy="24" r="3.4"/><circle cx="40" cy="44" r="3.4"/><path d="M30 30 L44 24 M44 24 L40 44 M40 44 L30 30"/><path d="M48 34 h26" stroke-dasharray="2 5"/><circle cx="84" cy="32" r="4.2" fill="#0C8F72" stroke="none"/><circle cx="96" cy="46" r="3.6" fill="#0C8F72" stroke="none"/><circle cx="90" cy="26" r="3" fill="#57D9B6" stroke="none"/></svg></div>
        <div class="edu-body"><h3>Peptides vs. GLP-1s — what's the difference?</h3><p>GLP-1s (like semaglutide and tirzepatide) are actually a type of peptide. "Peptide" is the broad category — short chains of amino acids that signal the body — and GLP-1 receptor agonists are one specific, well-known group within it. Whether any of them is appropriate for you is a clinical decision made by the physician.</p></div>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="edu-note">This section is general education, not medical advice. Whether any peptide is appropriate or safe for you is determined solely by an independent, licensed physician.</p>
    <div class="edu-cta"><a href="{{ cta_url }}" class="btn btn-primary btn-arrow">Start an online visit</a></div>
  </div>
</section>

